    {
      "tile_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select",
      "title": "The Relational Model and SELECT Basics",
      "summary": "Introduces the relational model — tables, rows and columns — and the SELECT … FROM statement that reads named columns from a table.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "sql.relational_model"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Relational Model",
          "title": "Tables, rows and columns",
          "lede": "Greenleaf Online Shop keeps its data in a relational database — a set of named tables, each holding one kind of thing.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s01.b01",
              "role": "intro",
              "text": "Greenleaf Online Shop stores its data in a relational database. Instead of one big spreadsheet, the data lives in separate tables — a customers table, an orders table — each holding its own kind of data.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model#tb1"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s01.b02",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "A relation — what SQL calls a table — holds data where each row (also called a record or tuple) represents one group of related data values. The columns are also called fields or attributes. In the customers table, each row is one customer; customer_id, name and city are its attributes.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model#tb2"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "sql.relational_model#f1",
                  "sql.relational_model#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "data_table",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s01.b03",
              "asset_ref": {
                "asset_id": "ds_customers",
                "variant_id": null,
                "columns": [
                  "customer_id",
                  "name",
                  "city"
                ],
                "max_rows": 4
              },
              "caption": "customers table — each row is one record/tuple; customer_id, name and city are fields/attributes.",
