        {
          "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s10",
          "type": "recap",
          "concept_ids": [
            "sql.relational_model",
            "sql.select_fundamentals"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Recap",
          "title": "What you've learned",
          "lede": "Tables, rows, columns — and the SELECT statement that reads them.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_relational_select.s10.b01",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "A relational database stores data in named tables. Each row represents one group of related values; each table covers one kind of thing only. SELECT … FROM is the shape every query builds on: FROM names the table to read, and the SELECT list names the columns — renamed or not — that come back out.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
