            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_sql_fund_alias_distinct.s02",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "sql.aliasing"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Aliasing",
          "title": "Column aliases and qualified column references",
          "lede": "Columns can be renamed in the SELECT list, and referenced with a table alias prefix to avoid ambiguity.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_alias_distinct.s02.b01",
              "role": "syntax",
              "text": "A column can be aliased by writing AS output_name after its expression in the SELECT list, exactly as you already saw with SELECT. A column can also be referenced as correlation.columnname, where correlation is a table name or an alias defined in the FROM clause — so with orders aliased to o, you can write o.customer_id instead of orders.customer_id. The correlation name and dot can be left off entirely when the column name is unique across every table in the query.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "sql.aliasing"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "sql.aliasing#tb3"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "sql.aliasing#f3",
                  "sql.aliasing#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_sql_fund_alias_distinct.s02.b02",
              "role": "practical_context",
              "text": "On a single table like SELECT o.order_id FROM orders AS o, qualifying a column is optional — the alias starts to matter once a query reads from more than one table and two of those tables share a column name. Qualifying which table's copy you mean is what keeps the query unambiguous.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "sql.aliasing"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "sql.aliasing#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
